          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong subtopos
            of a topos is a generalization of the concept of a subspace of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %strong subtopos
            is another topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} geometric embedding
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{E}
          %p
            If this is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+geometric+morphism"} open geometric morphism
            (or an
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential geometric morphism
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subtopos"} open subtopos
            (an
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+subtopos"} essential subtopos
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level of a topos
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#sheaves_localization_closure_and_reflection Sheaves, localization, closure and reflection
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            then subtoposes correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topologies"} Lawvere-Tierney topologies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localizations
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            as well as to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+operator"} universal closure operators
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %h3#for_classifying_toposes For classifying toposes
          %p
            Every Grothendieck topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is (equivalent to) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and it can be shown that subtoposes of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            correspond precisely to
            %strong deductively closed quotient theories
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            (
            %a{:href => "#Caramello09"}> Caramello (2009)
            ; thm. 3.6) i.e. passage to a subtopos corresponds to adding further geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            \- localizing geometrically amounts to theory refinement logically.
          %h3#LatticeOfSubtoposes The lattice of subtoposes
          %p
            The inclusions induce an ordering on the subtoposes of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            that makes them a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+algebra"} co-Heyting algebra
            structure i.e. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
            operator has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtraction"} subtraction
            operator. This corresponds to a dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            structure on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topologies"} Lawvere-Tierney topologies
          %p
            The lattice structure is further analyzed in (
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone02"}> Johnstone (2002)
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Caramello09"} Caramello (2009)
          #subtopos_meet.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_j(\mathcal{E})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Sh
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_k(\mathcal{E})
              be two subtoposes of a topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j,k
              the corresponding topologies with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\vee k
              their join in the lattice of topologies. Then the following holds:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mrow
                          %mi j
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_j(\mathcal{E})\wedge Sh_k(\mathcal{E})=Sh_{j\vee k}(\mathcal{E})=Sh_j(\mathcal{E})\cap Sh_k(\mathcal{E})
          %p
            (cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Johnstone02"} Johnstone (2002)
          #BooleantoposesAreAtoms.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atoms"} atoms
                in the lattice of subtoposes of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
                are precisely the two-valued
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean
                subtoposes
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Caramello09"}> Caramello (2009)
            ; prop. 10.1). This follows from the fact that two-valued and Boolean toposes are opposite extremes when it comes to dense subtoposes: in a two-valued topos every non-trivial subtopos is dense, whereas a Boolean topos has no non-trivial dense subtopos (cf. at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subtopos"} dense subtopos
            for further details).
